Patches applied to the pristine gdb-17.2 tarball by ../unpack.sh.

They apply with `patch -p1` from inside src/, in filename order, so the leading
number is the order.  Each one starts with a few lines of prose saying what it
does and why, then the diff -- the explanation is the point of keeping them as
separate files rather than editing src/ in place, which the next re-unpack
would silently discard.

The set, as built:

  0001-config-sub-likeos-triple.patch
        Teach the bundled config.sub about x86_64-*-likeos*.  There are two
        copies in the tree (src/config.sub and
        src/readline/readline/support/config.sub) and both are consulted.

  0002-gdb-configure-host-tgt.patch
        gdb/configure.host and gdb/configure.tgt entries selecting the native
        target module and OS ABI for the new triple.

  0003-gdb-likeos-nat-and-tdep.patch
        gdb/likeos-nat.c -- the native target_ops: attach/detach, register
        fetch/store, memory transfer, resume, wait, thread enumeration,
        breakpoint and watchpoint insertion.  Written against <sys/ptrace.h>;
        structurally modelled on the BSD nat modules, which are the closest
        analog to a small non-Linux Unix.
        gdb/likeos-tdep.c -- OS ABI registration, which is what points the
        architecture at solib-svr4 and relocates a position-independent
        executable.
        Two upstream files as well, both one-line-shaped fixes to paths that
        this target is the first to travel: gdb/inflow.c and gdb/posix-hdep.c.

  0004-gdbserver-likeos-low.patch
        gdbserver/likeos-low.cc -- the same primitives for the remote stub,
        plus the gdbserver/configure.srv entry that is what makes the tree
        build gdbserver at all.
